schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "AI Tools for Community Knowledge"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    An opinionated guide to the AI tools that help a small team build a
    knowledge commons that survives contributor turnover. Organized around
    one governing rule, "infrastructure should serve the work, not define
    it" — ask what job needs doing (capture, synthesize, search) before
    picking a tool. Walks through transcription (Otter.ai) for capture,
    retrieval-augmented generation (Google NotebookLM) for grounded
    synthesis, and local-first search (Obsidian + Smart Connections) for
    private, durable ownership, then closes on three guardrails: privacy,
    attribution, and naming one steward.
  audience: small community teams building a durable, AI-assisted knowledge base
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      cloud convenience (NotebookLM, Otter.ai) vs local ownership (Obsidian +
      Smart Connections) — article resolves this by routing sensitive or
      long-lived material to local tools and using cloud tools for
      lower-stakes synthesis and search.
    - >-
      polished AI writing reads as trustworthy whether or not it is accurate —
      the article treats this as more dangerous than a messy human note, not
      less, and insists AI drafts be marked distinctly from human-confirmed
      knowledge.
  related_concepts:
    - retrieval-augmented-generation
  related_resources:
    - Otter.ai
    - Google NotebookLM
    - Smart Connections
    - Quartz
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ai-community-tools.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-ae4676958221
  source_lineage: ai-community-tools.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - No people/community profiles or exact locations named in this article;
      privacy/consent is discussed as general advice about handling
      transcripts, not itself sensitive material — no high-risk trigger on
      the entry itself.
    - GravityDAO is mentioned only as an analogy ("the kind of practice
      GravityDAO has built its work around") rather than described in enough
      detail to warrant its own resource object; folded into this entry's
      summary instead.
